.TH CHTTPSVR_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_destroy \- destroy a chttpsvr handle and set it to CHTTPSVR_NULL
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_destroy(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Destroys the server that name holds and then sets name to
CHTTPSVR_NULL.

It drains every in-flight request and closes every idle connection
that is left.  Connections that wait without a thread are ended too:
a parked body or response is closed, a request that waits for body
memory is closed, and a streaming request that waits in the streaming
queue gets 503 Service Unavailable.  If this is the last active
server, the shared engine is stopped as well.  The listener socket is
closed along the way, so you don't have to call chttpsvr_stop first.

The destroy frees everything that the server allocated except for a
small part, about 100 bytes, of each sub-router that
chttpsvr_subrouter(3) created.  That part stays until the process
exits, so that a pointer to the sub-router is still safe to pass
after the destroy.  A server that a destructor or an atexit handler
of the application destroys after the exit-time cleanup of the
library has run frees that memory too, together with the bookkeeping
of the library, and its engine closes the logger of
chttpsvr_set_engine_logger(3) when it stops.

Once the teardown starts, the server takes no new request.  The
connections that wait idle for their next request are closed.  A
request that arrives later, on a keep-alive connection or pipelined
behind another request, runs no handler and gets 503 Service
Unavailable with Connection: close.  The response of a request whose
handler is already running carries Connection: close and ends its
connection.  The wait for the in-flight requests therefore ends when
the handlers that already run return, however many requests the
clients keep sending.  If handlers are still running after 30 s, the
library closes the connections they read from or write to, so that a
handler blocked on its client returns.

A second call on another copy of the same handle value is a FATAL
ERROR, whether it runs at the same time or later, after the first
call has completed.  The same holds for a call from inside a request
handler or a middleware of this server.  The library reports the
error at once with abort() and SIGABRT instead of a silent double
free or a hang.  To shut a server down from inside one of its own
handlers, destroy it from another thread or call
chttpsvr_engine_stop().

The macro is safe to call whether or not the shared engine is still
running.  It frees the engine reference of name, and if that was the
last reference an asynchronous engine stop can start.  It is also
safe when chttpsvr_engine_stop() has already forced the engine down
while name was started: that engine stop has then already quiesced
this server, so the call finds the teardown done and frees what is
left of the server.  If such a forced stop is still tearing this same
server down on its own background thread, the call waits for that
teardown to finish instead of racing it.

This macro only STARTS that asynchronous engine stop.  The destroy of
the reactor, the stop of the idle sweep and the close of the fallback
engine logger all run on a separate reaper thread after this call
returns, and every guarantee of this call is about name alone: its
listener is closed, its connections are closed and its worker pool is
drained.  Call chttpsvr_engine_wait() after it whenever that final
teardown has to finish before your next step.  Always call it before
you return from main() and before you unload this library from an
image that dlopen() loaded.  Call it as well before you tear down a
custom allocator installed with chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs(),
because the reaper frees the memory of the reactor and of the
fallback engine logger through that allocator.

The reaper thread itself doesn't need such a call.  An application
that destroys its last server and then calls neither
chttpsvr_engine_wait() nor a later chttpsvr_start() leaves no
unjoined thread and no thread stack mapping behind.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro evaluates name exactly once.  name must be a modifiable
lvalue, such as a variable or an array element, and with an
expression that has a side effect, such as handles[i++], exactly one
element is destroyed and cleared.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
